<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oaxaca</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oaxaca is a state of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the Pacific coast, with its capital at Oaxaca de Juárez. Its mountains and valleys were home to the Zapotec and Mixtec civilisations, including the city of Monte Albán, and the region was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> in the 15th century and by the Spanish in the 1520s. It has one of the largest Indigenous populations in Mexico.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oaxaca.jpg" alt="Oaxaca" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prsjl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oaxaqa_(41).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conquistadors spread out to subdue <b>Oaxaca</b>, Michoacán, Guatemala and the Gulf coast, while Nuño de Guzmán’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of New Galicia in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the following centuries the Zapotecs of <b>Oaxaca</b> built Monte Albán, from about 500 BCE, and recorded early dates and names in writing.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Countless place names in Mexico and Central America are Nahuatl: Mexico itself, Guatemala, Acapulco, <b>Oaxaca</b> and the volcano Popocatépetl, “smoking mountain”.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The heartland had no jade or obsidian of its own, and its people obtained them through networks reaching across Mesoamerica: obsidian from the highlands of central Mexico and Guatemala, jadeite from the Motagua valley, and iron ores for mirrors, some probably from <b>Oaxaca</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldaderas and camp followers, from young girls to middle-aged women and often of mestiza or indigenous origin, came from <b>Oaxaca</b>, Morelos, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, Sonora and elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-adelita">La Adelita</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom drew wealth from its position on trade routes between the Valley of Mexico, the Gulf and <b>Oaxaca</b>, and its market was frequented by merchants from many regions.</blockquote><a href="/state/coixtlahuaca">Coixtlahuaca</a></li></ul></section>
